  "html": "<!--\n  The pipeline conductor's board, as a DYNAMIC DASHBOARD CARD.\n\n  A FRAGMENT, not a document: ``dashboardDocument.ts`` parses this, binds the data,\n  prepends its own CSP and theme block and emits the doctype itself, so a wrapper here\n  would be reparented and the file would describe a document nobody serves.\n\n  Two halves, per ``dashboard/dynamic_cards.py``: this file is the ``html`` half and is\n  inert -- no script, no control, no outbound reference -- and every value arrives in the\n  ``data`` half, bound by ``data-dashboard-field`` (``dashboardDocument.ts`` sets each\n  bound element's ``textContent``). A field the data omits is bound to the EMPTY STRING,\n  which on a page of counts is indistinguishable from a real zero, so the flattener\n  ``pipeline_board_contract.panel_card_data`` writes a value for every field below and\n  writes words rather than a blank for a gap.\n\n  THE FIELD SET IS THE CONTRACT. ``test/test_dashboard_template_pipeline_board.py``\n  asserts this file's ``data-dashboard-field`` set equals ``panel_card_data``'s output\n  keys in BOTH directions (``test_the_card_binds_exactly_what_the_flattener_writes``),\n  and separately that every leaf of ``PipelineBoardPanel`` reaches one of them\n  (``test_every_contract_leaf_reaches_a_card_field``) -- so a field bound here with no writer, a key written with no binding, and a\n  contract leaf that reaches no field are each a failure rather than a blank cell.\n\n  NO DOTS IN A FIELD NAME. ``normalize_card``'s ``_FIELD_NAME`` admits\n  ``[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]{0,47}`` only, so the contract's dotted paths reach the card\n  with ``_`` for ``.`` (``meta.name`` -> ``meta_name``) and a list index as its own\n  segment (``columns[0].name`` -> ``column_0_head``). The whole rule is written out in\n  ``panel_card_data``'s docstring.\n\n  TWENTY-FOUR FIELDS, 4096 DATA BYTES, 8192 HTML BYTES. A card over any cap is dropped\n  WHOLE by the host, so a board with sixty items cannot be allowed to grow a field per\n  item: a column's rows arrive as ONE pre-wrap text field, and the flattener states its\n  own trimming in that text rather than silently showing a prefix.\n\n  NO ``content``, ``list-style`` or ``text-overflow`` DECLARATIONS. In card mode\n  ``dashboardDocument.ts`` deletes exactly those properties -- they make the browser draw\n  characters the backend's text scan never reads -- so a label put in ``::before`` would\n  be removed and leave the row unlabelled. Every label below is real markup text.\n-->\n<style>\n  :root{\n    --ink:    var(--text,   #f7f7f8);\n    --ink-2:  var(--muted,  #a1a1aa);\n    --edge:   var(--border, #27272a);\n    --sp1:4px; --sp2:8px; --sp3:12px; --sp4:16px;\n  }\n  *{box-sizing:border-box}\n  .wrap{display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--sp3);\n    font:14px/1.45 ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,\"Segoe UI\",Roboto,sans-serif;\n    color:var(--ink); font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}\n  .bar{display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:baseline; gap:var(--sp1) var(--sp3)}\n  .nm{font-weight:600; letter-spacing:-0.01em}\n  .dim{color:var(--ink-2); font-size:12px}\n  .lede{margin:0; font-size:15px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:-0.011em; max-width:74ch}\n  .cols{display:grid; gap:var(--sp2); grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(170px,1fr))}\n  .col{border:1px solid var(--edge); border-radius:8px; min-width:0; overflow:hidden}\n  .col > .hd{display:block; padding:var(--sp2) var(--sp3); border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);\n    font-size:12px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:0.02em; color:var(--ink)}\n  /* pre-WRAP, not pre-line. `pre-line` collapses white space and drops it where it sits\n       next to a forced break, so the four-space indent the flattener writes on an action line\n       and on the \"+N not shown\" line never rendered: both sat flush with the item rows they\n       belong under, and the only visual hierarchy in the column did not exist. `pre-wrap`\n       preserves the indent and still wraps a long line. */\n  .col > .rows{display:block; padding:var(--sp2) var(--sp3); margin:0;\n    font-size:12px; line-height:1.5; color:var(--ink-2); white-space:pre-wrap;\n    overflow-wrap:break-word; font-family:inherit}\n  .tiles{display:grid; gap:var(--sp2); grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(140px,1fr))}\n  .tile{border:1px solid var(--edge); border-radius:8px; padding:var(--sp2) var(--sp3)}\n  .tile .k{display:block; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:11px; font-weight:500;\n    text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em}\n  .tile .v{display:block; font-size:20px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:-0.02em;\n    line-height:1.2; margin-top:2px}\n  .tile .n{display:block; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:11.5px; line-height:1.35; margin-top:2px}\n  .foot{margin:0; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:12px}\n  /* The progress legend carries NO accent colour. This card declares zero interactive\n       elements and the host strips anything shaped like one, so accent-coloured text is a\n       promise the page cannot keep: a reader took the blue for a link, tried to follow it, and\n       nothing happened -- on every view, which costs trust in the numbers beside it. The\n       legend earns its weight from being the only full-width line above the columns. */\n  .legend{color:var(--ink); font-weight:500}\n</style>\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"bar\">\n    <span class=\"nm\" data-dashboard-field=\"meta_name\"></span>\n    <span class=\"dim\" data-dashboard-field=\"meta_when\"></span>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"bar\">\n    <span class=\"dim\" data-dashboard-field=\"omitted\"></span>\n    <span class=\"dim\" data-dashboard-field=\"contract_note\"></span>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"lede\" data-dashboard-field=\"lede\"></p>\n\n  <p class=\"dim legend\" data-dashboard-field=\"progress_legend\"></p>\n\n  <div class=\"cols\">\n    <section class=\"col\">\n      <span class=\"hd\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_0_head\"></span>\n      <p class=\"rows\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_0_rows\"></p>\n    </section>\n    <section class=\"col\">\n      <span class=\"hd\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_1_head\"></span>\n      <p class=\"rows\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_1_rows\"></p>\n    </section>\n    <section class=\"col\">\n      <span class=\"hd\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_2_head\"></span>\n      <p class=\"rows\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_2_rows\"></p>\n    </section>\n    <section class=\"col\">\n      <span class=\"hd\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_3_head\"></span>\n      <p class=\"rows\" data-dashboard-field=\"column_3_rows\"></p>\n    </section>\n  </div>\n\n  <!--\n    The tile LABELS are literal text, not bound fields. ``stats[].k`` is a closed\n    provider-side vocabulary -- ``_stats`` emits exactly items, entries, round, in that\n    order -- so a label is the same on every board ever published and belongs in the\n    inert half. The key still reaches the card: it is the field NAME each value is bound\n    by, and ``test_the_card_binds_a_value_and_a_note_for_every_stat_the_provider_emits``\n    builds a real board and asserts these three names against the provider's own keys,\n    so renaming a metric reddens here rather than leaving a tile bound to nothing.\n  -->\n  <div class=\"tiles\">\n    <div class=\"tile\">\n      <span class=\"k\">items on board</span>\n      <span class=\"v\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_items\"></span>\n      <span class=\"n\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_items_note\"></span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"tile\">\n      <span class=\"k\">log entries</span>\n      <span class=\"v\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_entries\"></span>\n      <span class=\"n\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_entries_note\"></span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"tile\">\n      <span class=\"k\">round</span>\n      <span class=\"v\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_round\"></span>\n      <span class=\"n\" data-dashboard-field=\"stat_round_note\"></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"foot\" data-dashboard-field=\"since\"></p>\n</div>\n"
}
